Approved: revision 18, 2026-08-04 (27 FRs / 10 NFRs / 50 ACs / 122 normative bullets).

Revision 18 was made during the design phase, in response to round 4 of the design review, and
carries design decision 3 of that round. It is ALIGNMENT, not new scope, and was approved without
an independent requirements review round under the requirement owner's standing rule.

What it changed: FR-23 asserted, as the cause of the condition it exists to handle, that because
IHttpContextAccessor is AsyncLocal-backed an HttpContext flows into work that outlives the response
and leaves HttpContext.RequestServices non-null over a disposed scope. A round-4 reviewer compiled
and ran an ASP.NET probe and disproved it: the built-in HttpContextAccessor holds its AsyncLocal
over a SHARED HOLDER OBJECT and clears that holder at end of request, so an already-captured flow
sees a null HttpContext. The deferred-work case is therefore FR-24.2 (no ambient offered), which
AC-29 expressly forbids - so AC-29 was unsatisfiable by a correct implementation, and it is the AC
the design's usability probe is justified by.

FR-23's rule, its Warning, its once-per-Brighter-container-per-provider-implementation-type latch
(D19), and its evaluation order relative to FR-24.4 and FR-24.2 are ALL UNCHANGED. Only the
population it applies to is stated correctly: a provider that CAPTURES a resolution source rather
than reading it afresh on every ask - an AsyncLocal-backed provider for non-ASP.NET hosts (NFR-7,
AC-35), one that stores HttpContext.RequestServices at construction, or a custom accessor that does
not clear itself. FR-23 now names that population and states in a parenthesis that the built-in
accessor is NOT in it. FR-23's example and AC-29's Given are re-sited onto that shape; AC-29's
Then, its count, its latch clause and its second-Send branch are untouched.

Why no review round: the standing rule is that alignment needs none and an amendment that is more
than alignment gets one. This adds no rule, no AC and no scope; it removes a false codebase claim
and repoints one example and one Given at a scenario that can actually arise. Counts unchanged at
27/10/50. The empirical basis is recorded in review-design.md (reviewer 0072, finding 1, score 85)
and in PROMPT.md section 17.6.

This supersedes the revision-17 approval, which this file recorded. Revisions 16 and 17 were made
in response to round 2 of the design review: revision 16 carried the AddBrighterAspNetCoreScopes ->
AddBrighterRequestScope rename (alignment) and FR-22.4 with its new AC-50 (new scope, and so it had
its own review round - 11 findings, 9 at or above threshold, none Critical); revision 17 closed
those nine plus two below-threshold items. That round's owner decision - FR-22.4 covers BOTH
registration orderings, so a TryAddSingleton-time-only detector is not sufficient - still stands and
is carried in FR-22.4 and in the design's sixth scope-configuration rule.
